Hmm, but there's a subtlety — for round 1, the early return at 136-140 handles it first. With the flip, round 1 returns before reaching `if (!(round === 2))`. Then for round 2, we fall into the happy path flat. For rounds ≥ 3, we return early with the final text. That works.

But wait — what about the `round === 1` block? It also ends in return and has statements dangling after... The rule flagged only 141:9, not 136. Why? Maybe because the `if (round === 1)` block has dangling statements that are themselves guarded (an if), so the rule doesn't fire, or maybe the rule fires on the LAST such if before unguarded tail statements, or counts dangling statements outside any if... Line 151-152 are the only unguarded statements. The rule fires on the if immediately preceding flat statements — i.e., the last if. Presumably the rule only fires on the if whose body ends with return AND has statements directly after it in the same block. Both `if (round===1)` and `if (round===2)` have that property... but only 141 was flagged. Maybe the rule fires once per function on the closest if to the tail. Whatever — I need to fix 141 without introducing a new violation at 136.

Hmm, if I flip 141, the new structure:

```js
