firstRoundToolResult = ...;
yield {tool_use, call-2};
yield {stop: tool_use};
```

Now `if (round === 1)` still ends with return and has statements after (the `if (!(round===2))` block) — would the rule fire on 136 now? The rule apparently fired on the if followed by 2 flat statements. After my flip, `if (round === 1)` is followed by an `if` statement, not flat yields. The original firing at 141 said "2 statement(s) dangle after it". If the rule only fires when flat statements (not blocks/ifs) dangle, then `if (round===1)` followed by another if-statement wouldn't fire. But that's uncertain.

